00:52:31.491 --> 00:52:31.991
for them,

00:52:31.991 --> 00:52:33.752
the past is what occurred yesterday.

00:52:34.392 --> 00:52:37.494
And they often see the past as not being relevant.

00:52:37.934 --> 00:52:38.634
And yet I would argue,

00:52:38.654 --> 00:52:40.175
certainly in a Canadian context,

00:52:40.375 --> 00:52:44.818
if you want to understand Bill C-51 and some of the changes that were brought in...

00:52:45.557 --> 00:52:46.618
You have to know what happened in the

00:52:47.539 --> 00:52:49.881
1970s with the disruption operations.

00:52:58.828 --> 00:52:59.869
In the 70s you have,

00:52:59.870 --> 00:53:00.230
you know,

00:53:00.230 --> 00:53:02.191
right after the October crisis,

00:53:02.691 --> 00:53:03.792
you have a lot of fear.

00:53:04.621 --> 00:53:06.763
That the FLQ is going to do something more.

00:53:07.344 --> 00:53:11.869
And there's a lot of fear over how the RCMP is going to properly track people.

00:53:11.929 --> 00:53:13.050
How can we stay ahead of this?

00:53:13.110 --> 00:53:14.272
How can we know what's happening?

00:53:14.812 --> 00:53:16.494
But in their attempts to stay ahead of it,

00:53:17.095 --> 00:53:18.176
they create some of it.

00:53:24.561 --> 00:53:27.325
They break into a party Quebecois headquarters,

00:53:27.346 --> 00:53:29.088
they steal their memberships list,

00:53:29.168 --> 00:53:30.290
they copy it,

00:53:31.652 --> 00:53:34.197
they break into a small news agency,

00:53:34.357 --> 00:53:35.659
steal their files,

00:53:36.380 --> 00:53:38.584
they famously burn a barn.

00:53:42.201 --> 00:53:50.046
So there was supposedly going to be a meeting between members of the Black Panthers and supposedly FLQ members.

00:53:50.607 --> 00:53:53.429
The police wanted to plant a microphone to record it,

00:53:53.449 --> 00:53:54.149
but they couldn't,

00:53:54.589 --> 00:53:55.850
for technological reasons,

00:53:55.910 --> 00:53:56.991
couldn't put the mic in.

00:53:57.111 --> 00:53:59.633
And so they actually destroyed the barn.

00:53:59.634 --> 00:54:03.597
They actually burned the barn to stop the meeting from taking place.

00:54:03.937 --> 00:54:07.000
And then they destroyed almost all of the files as well.

00:54:07.180 --> 00:54:11.324
So the reason we know about the things in Quebec is because you had the Keeble inquiry,

00:54:11.885 --> 00:54:13.386
you had the MacDonald commission,

00:54:13.767 --> 00:54:14.908
where some of this came out.

00:54:28.269 --> 00:54:32.611
One of the famous infiltration cases in the 70s is Carole Deveaux,

00:54:32.811 --> 00:54:33.651
alias Poupette,

00:54:33.691 --> 00:54:36.312
who infiltrated the FLQ for five years.

00:54:42.554 --> 00:54:46.515
She participated in organizing a qualified flight.

00:54:46.516 --> 00:54:51.157
She participated in an attack on the fire bomb against the company Briggs.

00:54:53.705 --> 00:54:57.353
She participated in an extortion attempt with the federal government.

00:54:57.754 --> 00:55:02.644
And that's just for this informant from the Montreal Police.

00:55:06.945 --> 00:55:08.647
In addition to all this,

00:55:08.648 --> 00:55:09.347
at the same time,

00:55:09.348 --> 00:55:19.878
the GRC wrote a communique of the FLQ to invite the FLQists to continue the armed struggle after Pierre Vallière said,

00:55:19.879 --> 00:55:22.400
no,
I withdraw from all this to go to the

00:55:22.761 --> 00:55:23.462
Parti Québécois.

00:55:23.902 --> 00:55:29.147
So the police encourages the FLQ to continue in their radicalism.

00:55:35.545 --> 00:55:44.692
They worried that the FLQ would infiltrate the Parti Québécois and that it would be harder for the RCMP to monitor them there because the Parti Québécois was legal.

00:55:46.425 --> 00:55:47.126
Claude Marin,

00:55:47.706 --> 00:55:53.711
who was the number two man in the Parti Québécois government.

00:55:53.791 --> 00:55:56.974
Only René Lévesque was above him in the party,

00:55:57.074 --> 00:56:03.980
and he was the architect of the whole strategy of the referendum that was held in 1980.

00:56:04.060 --> 00:56:08.243
And it turned out that Claude Marin was a paid informant of the RCMP.

00:56:13.977 --> 00:56:16.278
They were doing lots of other things outside of Quebec.

00:56:17.098 --> 00:56:18.439
They got a lot less attention,

00:56:18.479 --> 00:56:18.979
but we,

00:56:19.259 --> 00:56:19.680
in a sense,

00:56:19.680 --> 00:56:23.281
will never know because they destroyed almost all of the files.

00:56:23.421 --> 00:56:26.723
One of the reasons they did this is because they'd seen what had happened in the U.S.

00:56:27.203 --> 00:56:30.624
with the FBI's COINTELPRO files becoming public.

00:56:38.073 --> 00:56:38.614
Pierre Trudeau,

00:56:38.774 --> 00:56:40.696
in one of its very last acts,

00:56:40.736 --> 00:56:41.217
in fact,

00:56:41.217 --> 00:56:42.578
before going out of office,

00:56:42.898 --> 00:56:45.662
passed the Canadian Security Intelligence Service Act,

00:56:45.663 --> 00:56:46.523
the CSIS Act,

00:56:47.444 --> 00:56:47.804
which,

00:56:47.804 --> 00:56:48.725
among other things,

00:56:49.706 --> 00:56:58.336
separated the security service out of the RCMP so that you had policing and security intelligence as separate bodies.

00:57:03.973 --> 00:57:05.334
And that was one of the ironies,

00:57:05.454 --> 00:57:06.154
is that you have

00:57:06.534 --> 00:57:09.595
CSIS created in 1984,

00:57:09.615 --> 00:57:13.217
largely because of disruption operations carried out by the RCMP,

00:57:13.497 --> 00:57:14.217
mainly in Quebec,

00:57:14.337 --> 00:57:14.858
but elsewhere,

00:57:14.859 --> 00:57:16.818
that went horribly wrong,

00:57:17.639 --> 00:57:18.619
inquiries.

00:57:19.059 --> 00:57:21.780
And so then you have CSIS created as a result,

00:57:21.900 --> 00:57:22.400
and then

00:57:22.741 --> 00:57:29.784
Bill C-51 gives CSIS powers to carry out disruption operations of the type that were illegal in the 70s,

00:57:30.384 --> 00:57:32.625
but now have the blessing of the state.

00:57:44.956 --> 00:57:50.478
The SCRS did not expect the Parliament to give it the authorization to break the law.

00:57:50.538 --> 00:57:50.998
In fact,

00:57:50.998 --> 00:57:57.561
there are several well-documented cases of SCRS informants who have been given provocation.

00:58:00.642 --> 00:58:02.923
There is the case of Marc-André Boivin,

00:58:03.263 --> 00:58:13.628
who was a union activist and informant for the SCRS for 15 years and who was sentenced to 15 months in prison for his participation in bomb attacks.

00:58:16.982 --> 00:58:21.465
The oppression is just mounting and mounting and mounting and the time to fight is now.

00:58:22.606 --> 00:58:26.269
White power There is the case of Grant Bristow,

00:58:26.329 --> 00:58:28.871
who is a co-founder of the Heritage Fund,

00:58:28.872 --> 00:58:32.454
the white supremacist organization that was the most dangerous.

00:58:32.454 --> 00:58:34.656
and the most powerful in Canada in the 90s,

00:58:34.657 --> 00:58:46.307
and who led a campaign of disturbance and sabotage against anti-racist activists who mobilized against white supremacists.

00:58:46.308 --> 00:58:55.755
He did all this without ever being accused.

00:59:06.572 --> 00:59:09.673
And there is also the case of Joseph Gilbrou,

00:59:09.833 --> 00:59:11.054
alias Youssef Muammar,

00:59:11.214 --> 00:59:12.494
alias Abu Djihad,

00:59:12.554 --> 00:59:22.057
who was depicted as a leader of the Muslim community and who sent death threats,

00:59:22.517 --> 00:59:23.518
terrorist attacks,

00:59:23.519 --> 00:59:30.080
especially against the Montreal metro for years and who was never worried because he was protected by the SCRS.

00:59:37.172 --> 00:59:50.892
How many other cases of SCRS informants have acted as provocateur agents that we will never discover because they are protected by the highest authorities?

01:00:00.584 --> 01:00:00.904
Okay,

01:00:00.904 --> 01:00:02.706
do you see this?

01:00:03.006 --> 01:00:04.627
Because Home Depot is just there.

01:00:04.767 --> 01:00:05.568
We're gonna go there.

01:00:06.068 --> 01:00:06.488
How much,

01:00:06.488 --> 01:00:07.329
what do you need now?

01:00:09.831 --> 01:00:10.331
Glue,

01:00:10.892 --> 01:00:11.572
nails,

01:00:13.534 --> 01:00:15.435
a couple more clocks.

01:00:16.996 --> 01:00:18.397
Oh,
they didn't have the clocks there?

01:00:19.858 --> 01:00:20.999
They were all digital.

01:00:23.340 --> 01:00:25.141
They had taken some steps.

01:00:25.901 --> 01:00:31.263
They had been involved in the purchase of these materials to eventually build these bombs.

01:00:31.764 --> 01:00:38.106
They played a role in their construction over a few days at a hotel out in Tawasin.

01:00:41.568 --> 01:00:44.909
It doesn't seem real.

01:00:45.450 --> 01:00:46.850
I have that same feeling.

01:00:46.851 --> 01:00:47.290
It's surreal.

01:00:47.290 --> 01:00:48.311
Yeah,
it's not that it's not...

01:00:48.331 --> 01:00:49.631
I feel like I'm in a movie.

01:00:49.632 --> 01:00:50.332
I really do.

01:00:51.236 --> 01:00:53.098
Now it's really happening,

01:00:53.418 --> 01:00:53.918
you know.

01:00:54.319 --> 01:00:57.041
Before it was just talk,

01:00:58.242 --> 01:00:59.763
it wasn't reality,

01:01:00.303 --> 01:01:00.803
you know.

01:01:01.825 --> 01:01:02.325
Now

01:01:02.946 --> 01:01:05.888
I am stuck with this.

01:01:11.913 --> 01:01:12.413
Bill C-51,

01:01:13.334 --> 01:01:15.636
one of its extremely problematic innovations,

01:01:15.996 --> 01:01:20.300
was to introduce this category of...

01:01:20.580 --> 01:01:28.423
activities that undermine the security of Canada as the basis for information sharing exercises between national security agencies.

01:01:28.824 --> 01:01:37.988
And within the list of activities that were listed as activities that undermine the security of Canada were things that imperil critical infrastructure,

01:01:38.548 --> 01:01:39.048
i.e.

01:01:39.746 --> 01:01:40.907
Represented as pipelines,

01:01:41.327 --> 01:01:46.289
activities that undermine the sovereignty or imperil the sovereignty of Canada,

01:01:47.029 --> 01:01:48.110
Indigenous resistance.

01:01:49.670 --> 01:01:52.652
If you protested the Enbridge Northern Gateway Pipeline,

01:01:52.912 --> 01:01:56.133
Canada's spy agency may have you on file.

01:02:00.222 --> 01:02:05.406
There aren't very many organized environmentalists in Canada who don't think that they're under surveillance or are very,

01:02:05.486 --> 01:02:10.029
very concerned about their sort of classification as a national security threat.

01:02:11.450 --> 01:02:12.490
There's a reason for that.

01:02:12.570 --> 01:02:13.070
I mean,

01:02:13.411 --> 01:02:14.872
this has already been established.

01:02:15.512 --> 01:02:21.776
in relation to some of the demonstrations that CSIS is interested in them,

01:02:21.796 --> 01:02:23.697
that the RCMP are interested in them,

01:02:23.977 --> 01:02:25.057
that they're being profiled,

01:02:25.117 --> 01:02:25.838
all of these things.

01:02:25.998 --> 01:02:27.819
That's very clearly the case.

01:02:28.359 --> 01:02:36.964
Questions are currently being raised about the role of Canada's spy agency after British Columbia's Civil Liberties Association released thousands of pages of documents.

01:02:37.364 --> 01:02:40.886
The group alleges CSIS monitored the activities of Indigenous groups.

01:02:41.410 --> 01:02:47.755
And environmental protesters of the Northern Gateway Pipeline and shared some of the information it collected with oil companies.

01:02:51.637 --> 01:02:53.098
BCCLA's files,

01:02:53.138 --> 01:02:54.980
the so-called protest papers,

01:02:55.380 --> 01:03:04.126
revealed very disturbing evidence of the use of mass surveillance against Indigenous land and water protectors,

01:03:04.586 --> 01:03:09.830
surveillance that was shared with the oil industry in the U.S.

01:03:13.766 --> 01:03:28.755
We've seen police agencies being hired by extractive corporations to serve effectively as their private enforcement mechanism for their projects of depredations of Indigenous lands.

01:03:30.456 --> 01:03:41.342
And so this isn't a hypothetical when we are considering the use of these apparatuses of national security to repress opposition.

01:03:41.702 --> 01:03:47.126
to what is in fact the greatest threat to our collective human security,

01:03:47.526 --> 01:03:52.910
which is the catastrophe of climate and ecological breakdown,

01:03:53.230 --> 01:03:54.591
which we are confronting.

01:03:55.151 --> 01:04:05.258
And yet the state continues to prioritize the interests of those who are advancing this apocalypse rather than those who are opposing it.

01:04:13.894 --> 01:04:19.076
Climate change is happening faster than we'll be able to deal with in a few years,

01:04:19.077 --> 01:04:20.577
like if we don't get a hold of this.

01:04:21.017 --> 01:04:25.819
So what do you do when you're a government that actually doesn't care about that,

01:04:25.859 --> 01:04:28.540
but only cares about power and wealth in the here and now?

01:04:29.281 --> 01:04:35.504
You have to manufacture some reason to maintain the status quo.

01:04:35.964 --> 01:04:36.504
So what do you do?

01:04:37.204 --> 01:04:38.025
What do they always do?

01:04:38.085 --> 01:04:39.865
They manufacture some kind of threat.

01:04:48.526 --> 01:04:54.650
Now people who go and march in protective old growth forests or protect water,

01:04:54.990 --> 01:04:56.772
they're eco-terrorists.

01:04:57.232 --> 01:04:59.073
And even the terminology has changed.

01:04:59.293 --> 01:05:00.914
Not environmentalists anymore.

01:05:01.215 --> 01:05:02.355
Not conservationists.

01:05:02.635 --> 01:05:03.156
You're an eco-

01:05:03.556 --> 01:05:04.376
terrorist.

01:05:05.157 --> 01:05:06.557
Because if you're a terrorist,

01:05:06.957 --> 01:05:08.278
that means we can monitor,

01:05:08.398 --> 01:05:08.958
surveil,

01:05:09.118 --> 01:05:10.059
criminalize,

01:05:10.219 --> 01:05:11.479
do whatever we want to you,

01:05:11.859 --> 01:05:19.902
and portray that as a threat to public safety and national security to justify what we're doing to maintain the status quo.

01:05:34.322 --> 01:05:44.389
You'll never know the true form of terror until you're holding literal ticking time bomb in your hand.

01:05:45.250 --> 01:05:46.371
And to this day,

01:05:47.752 --> 01:05:57.819
the sound of a ticking clock sets me and my wife into a state of panic and we're on drugs and treatment for PTSD.

01:05:57.879 --> 01:06:00.461
It's too late for that,

01:06:00.462 --> 01:06:00.962
Anna.

01:06:01.602 --> 01:06:03.124
It took about three days for them to,

01:06:03.845 --> 01:06:04.345
in effect,

01:06:04.486 --> 01:06:06.329
glue some nails into a pressure cooker.

01:06:06.369 --> 01:06:08.232
That was about as far as they could get.

01:06:11.016 --> 01:06:12.318
Once their part there was done,

01:06:12.378 --> 01:06:13.680
off those devices went to...

01:06:14.862 --> 01:06:16.223
what they believed to be the terrorists,

01:06:16.303 --> 01:06:18.704
but were of course officers who sort of fixed them up,

01:06:19.584 --> 01:06:27.727
put in a tiny bit of C4 along with modeling clay from Michael's to make it look like there was a whole bunch of C4 in there,

01:06:28.528 --> 01:06:31.629
and then off they went to Victoria and then they actually planted the devices.

01:06:43.695 --> 01:06:46.196
The police provocation benefits the police first and foremost.

01:06:46.256 --> 01:06:50.137
It allows us to justify budgets,

01:06:50.797 --> 01:06:52.758
to come and say,

01:06:52.878 --> 01:06:56.079
look at the terrorist villains we've caught.

01:06:56.119 --> 01:06:58.320
That's why we need more power,

01:06:58.340 --> 01:07:02.481
that's why we need more ferocious anti-terrorist laws,

01:07:02.521 --> 01:07:05.402
while the police were behind all this,

01:07:05.502 --> 01:07:06.683
orchestrated everything.

01:07:11.023 --> 01:07:14.088
If you can go out and get a successful prosecution on a robbery case,

01:07:14.509 --> 01:07:17.374
that's a police car and a cop and some guy doing an investigation.

01:07:18.195 --> 01:07:19.536
You have to do wiretap,

01:07:19.616 --> 01:07:21.717
you have to do sophisticated surveillance,

01:07:21.797 --> 01:07:22.617
data monitoring,

01:07:22.958 --> 01:07:24.218
and the related kind of high-tech,

01:07:24.338 --> 01:07:25.659
sophisticated policing.

01:07:26.259 --> 01:07:26.780
That's money.

01:07:27.040 --> 01:07:27.920
That's resources.

01:07:27.960 --> 01:07:29.241
That's got to come from somewhere.

01:07:29.641 --> 01:07:32.563
And the way you get it there is by having successful prosecutions.

01:07:33.283 --> 01:07:38.226
You can't run a case for two years and show nothing for it and think you're going to get a budget the following year.

01:07:38.926 --> 01:07:47.871
So I think there's a massive incentive on the part of the enforcement agencies who want to try to upgrade to higher-level equipment to target crimes.

01:07:48.031 --> 01:07:51.253
alleged crimes or manufactured crimes to make sure that that budget gets met.

01:07:57.476 --> 01:08:07.522
There's huge money involved in the national security industry and tools of policing and everything that you can think of from malware

01:08:07.542 --> 01:08:13.125
to drones it's all going into the funnels of policing or national security.

01:08:13.665 --> 01:08:16.587
It's a massive massive industry and they have these

01:08:17.387 --> 01:08:36.244
astonishing trade shows where you find out you know the tools of the future and what they're planning to empower you to do the old issues of human sources infiltrating and and into groups and so on i think we're

01:08:36.264 --> 01:08:43.290
probably going to see less of that and we're going to see a great deal more of scrutiny of the data sphere.

01:08:48.691 --> 01:08:52.287
The technological advances in surveillance

01:08:53.535 --> 01:09:11.307
And the capacity that now exists to collect and to analyze that so-called big data have the potential for creating these huge databases and increasingly sophisticated algorithms

01:09:11.467 --> 01:09:18.732
that can develop profiles of the kind of people that might be radicalized or the kind of people that might pose a threat,

01:09:18.973 --> 01:09:20.073
which can be very,

01:09:20.173 --> 01:09:21.014
very dangerous.

01:09:26.671 --> 01:09:29.254
Even if all of our communications are intercepted,

01:09:29.854 --> 01:09:35.740
the consequences differ greatly depending on how we connect to certain variables.

01:09:36.060 --> 01:09:37.201
What religious beliefs,

01:09:37.321 --> 01:09:38.422
political beliefs,

01:09:39.083 --> 01:09:39.443
race,

01:09:39.443 --> 01:09:40.044
ethnicity,

01:09:40.464 --> 01:09:41.165
sexuality,

01:09:41.886 --> 01:09:50.514
and certain groups that have historically been marginalized end up disproportionately experiencing the consequences of that surveillance.

01:09:54.183 --> 01:09:55.204
We're heading towards that,

01:09:55.244 --> 01:09:55.744
I think,

01:09:55.764 --> 01:10:02.788
where people are going to be targeted simply because they fit certain characteristics that a computer program has flagged up.

01:10:02.789 --> 01:10:07.491
And then they'll get the really intrusive surveillance coming in after that.

01:10:15.056 --> 01:10:19.158
We've come a very long way in a short amount of time in terms of policing powers.

01:10:20.507 --> 01:10:21.288
What you could do,

01:10:21.568 --> 01:10:22.370
for example,

01:10:22.890 --> 01:10:27.596
with the powers that the CSE now has for what are called active cyber,

01:10:27.977 --> 01:10:29.078
we can cyber attack you,

01:10:30.660 --> 01:10:31.160
right?

01:10:31.361 --> 01:10:33.264
We can do things that,

01:10:33.284 --> 01:10:33.784
again,

01:10:33.804 --> 01:10:35.286
simply didn't exist before.

01:10:41.635 --> 01:10:44.179
This is all just like forced on us.

01:10:45.200 --> 01:10:46.302
We have no option,

01:10:46.303 --> 01:10:47.383
we have no way out,

01:10:47.384 --> 01:10:54.773
we have to do it.

01:10:54.774 --> 01:10:55.855
We're in Victoria,

01:10:56.796 --> 01:10:58.038
choosing a target.

01:10:59.063 --> 01:11:04.047
We drove around for like a long time because I didn't have a plan.

01:11:05.268 --> 01:11:06.429
The thought came to me,

01:11:06.889 --> 01:11:09.351
what if we just blew up a government building?

01:11:10.032 --> 01:11:11.673
Then we wouldn't have to hurt anyone.

01:11:12.874 --> 01:11:14.416
Would your boss be angry?

01:11:15.637 --> 01:11:18.299
Because I promised him a high kill count.

01:11:19.440 --> 01:11:20.941
No,
he's not going to be angry.

01:11:21.101 --> 01:11:22.282
This is your plan,

01:11:22.382 --> 01:11:22.923
he said.

01:11:29.199 --> 01:11:30.140
He asked me too,

01:11:30.240 --> 01:11:30.740
he said,

01:11:31.040 --> 01:11:35.883
do you want maximum damage to this building or to people?

01:11:36.043 --> 01:11:36.723
And I said,

01:11:36.823 --> 01:11:37.584
to the building,

01:11:37.664 --> 01:11:38.344
to the building.

01:11:39.525 --> 01:11:41.586
And by the grace of God,

01:11:42.626 --> 01:11:44.307
it was accepted as a mission.

01:11:46.769 --> 01:11:49.530
He pointed to some bushes and he said,

01:11:50.170 --> 01:11:51.491
that's where you're going to plant them.

01:11:53.452 --> 01:11:53.953
Stand by,

